Z||000009||MooCow||08-19-2001||11:37 PM||||The filter sits right where the stock on does but not in the canister, so its right under the deck vent basically.  Its just like buying one from Chris West only I did this for free.<P>Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||08-20-2001||01:05 AM||||You cold make a shield that kept rainwater away the shield far enough away from the filter so it doesn't become a restriction.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||08-20-2001||11:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MooCow:<BR><B>The filter sits right where the stock on does but not in the canister, so its right under the deck vent basically.  Its just like buying one from Chris West only I did this for free.<P>Thanks Ken</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, if it's where the factory air cleaner canister sits (inside the engine compartment and under the engine vent) you've got 2 problems.  1: you're breathing hot engine compartment air and that'll kill your performance and possible lead to detonation. and 2:  rain is going to flood down onto your filter and soak it.  in that posistion, yes, you do have something to worry about.  I've even had my stock air cleaner get soaked from water sitting on top of the air cleaner lid and leaking down past the hold down nut. (Nylon washer fixed that problem).<P>The cone filter should be in the fender well where the water diverter box was located.  That gets you outside of the hot engine compartment and out of direct rainfall.  Then you only have to worry about water being sucked in from the fender vent, which should be negligable.  You might also want to seal the hole in the fender where air intake pipe sticks through to keep any hot air from leaking into the fender well. <P>For my system, I removed the water diverter box and replaced it and it's connectors with an off the shelf flexible intake duct available at Pep Boys.  It attaches to the base of the OEM air cleaner canister and runs to the fender grill vent.  At the fender vent, the rubber fitting behind the grill is stretched over a 3" dia. PVC pipe section that is about 3" long.  The flexible duct then clamps onto the other side of the PVC pipe.  All very tidy - and I'm breathing 100% outside air, not underhood air.  And I can change my air filter without having to take off my left rear tire!  No, it didn't give me any noticeable performance gain.  But the sound alone was worth it.  ||||reg||4
Z||000012||Formula88||08-20-2001||11:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MooCow:<BR><B>The filter sits right where the stock on does but not in the canister, so its right under the deck vent basically.  Its just like buying one from Chris West only I did this for free.<P>Thanks Ken</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, correct me if I'm wrong, but the West Coast Fiero intakes stick through the hole in the fender well, so the filter is in the fender well where the water diverter box was, not in the engine compartment.  It sounds like you just made your pipe too short.||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||08-20-2001||12:39 PM||||The casue for the smoke in the morning and after it rains is simple:<BR>  Until the exaust gas reaches a high enough tempeture to evaporate the condensation (in the morning its the same phenomenon that casues dew) that forms on the cold pipe you will see that.  It also tends to be more pronounced becasue the air is heavy at those times also i.e. when after rain it is humid and in the AM the air has not been heated.  Once the sun comes up and the air is warmer you usually do not see this (also since the condensation on the pipe tends to evaporate with warmer temps!).<P>While I doubt that a little water would hurt I think the post about it dumping into the filter is correct.  In that placement I can see water being able to run into the intake even with the engine off.  Does not sound to me like a fun think to have in the morning.||||reg||1
Z||000014||MooCow||08-21-2001||01:20 AM||||I don't think that I am sucking in hot air at all.  The reason I say that is because my car runs very cold now.  It used to site 1 line away from 220 and now it will sit at only 1 line in from 0.  It seems if I was sucking in hot air the engine would become more hotter and that is not the case.  My filter is comming right of the stock tube.  All you do is use the top of your air canister and then the very bottom part break off and put the filter in the middle along with the sensor.  I can feel a difernce but since I am affraid to ruin anything I guess I may have to go back to normal.<P>Thanks everyone, Ken||||reg||1
